GETTY(8)                       P32W Unix                        GETTY(8)

NAME
       getty - set up a terminal and ask for a login name

SYNOPSIS
       /etc/getty tty

DESCRIPTION
       getty is started by init(8), one on each terminal; it is not
       meant to be run by hand. tty is the terminal's name without
       /dev/: tty0 or tty1.

       getty opens the terminal (/dev/tty0 for tty0) as its standard
       input, output and error, which makes it the session's
       controlling terminal, and sets sane modes for logging in:
       echo on, carriage return read as newline (CRMOD), erase ^H,
       line kill ^U, no cbreak or raw mode. Then it prints

              P32W Unix (tty0)

              login:

       and reads a name. An empty line asks again. With a name it
       executes /bin/login name tty, which asks for the password;
       see login(1).

       There is no speed setting: the serial lines run at whatever
       speed the hardware is set to.

EXIT STATUS
       0 at end of file on the terminal (init then starts a new
       getty); 1 if tty is missing, the terminal cannot be opened,
       or /bin/login cannot be executed.

FILES
       /dev/tty0, /dev/tty1
                      the terminals
       /bin/login     run with the name

SEE ALSO
       init(8), login(1), stty(1), rc(8)

BUGS
       A name longer than 63 characters is cut short. There is no
       /etc/gettytab: the banner and the modes are fixed in the
       program.
